                      </span>
                    </div>
                    <p className="m-0 pt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
                      Found in the nucleus; adds mass but no charge. Number of
                      neutrons is A − Z.
                    </p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>

              {/* Cathode Ray Experiment */}
              <div className="my-8 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-800/40 p-6">
                <h4 className="m-0 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
                  <Zap className="h-5 w-5 text-green-500" />
                  How cathode rays revealed the electron
                </h4>
                <ul className="m-0 space-y-3 pl-5 text-sm list-disc">
                  <li>
                    A high voltage across a gas-discharge tube produces a beam
                    of rays from the cathode (negative electrode).
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    The rays bend toward the positive plate in an electric
                    field and in a magnetic field — proof they carry{" "}
                    <strong>negative charge</strong>.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    The charge-to-mass ratio (e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C/kg) was the
                    same for every gas and electrode material. Electrons are
                    therefore a universal part of all atoms.
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    Millikan's oil-drop experiment (1909) measured the
                    elementary charge: −1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.
                  </li>
                </ul>
              </div>

              {/* Properties Table */}
              <div className="my-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-700">
